Challenge a fitted model with evidence it did not fit (@inquiry-model-checks: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../metrology/uncertainty/Model-discrepancy-and-robustness.lingua#r_13PJ61YH29120EKV42ZT1SQTG3") { r_000F183C16A66438AC6F589418
A model can fit its defining observations and still make wrong predictions elsewhere. Fitting chooses parameters to reproduce some evidence; checking asks whether independent or differently structured evidence agrees with the model under its stated uncertainty. Reuse [Connect chemical composition to a signal and expose missing information](../../chemistry/core/Connect-chemical-composition-to-a-signal-and-expose-missing-information.lingua#r_0E739CDF730C0B949DBB0338EF) and [Compare thermal alternatives and the evidence needed to choose](../../engineering/core/Compare-thermal-alternatives-and-the-evidence-needed-to-choose.lingua#r_00B7A969633B37AA7737D882CB) for observations that cannot separate parameters without additional information.

Consider an original noiseless calibration thought experiment. Let t be temperature expressed numerically in °C over a small stated range and r its indication, also in °C. Model A is r=t. Model B is r=t+κ(t−20)(t−22), with κ=0.1 K⁻¹ when the differences are interpreted in kelvins. Both reproduce reference pairs (20,20) and (22,22). At reference temperature 23 °C, A predicts 23.0 °C and B predicts 23.3 °C. Both are monotone on [20,23] because B's derivative is 1+0.1(2t−42), ranging from 0.8 to 1.4. The example is a rival model construction, not a claim about a specific sensor.

The calibration points alone cannot choose A over B. A new reference point at 23 °C could distinguish their predictions only if the reference, indication, stability and procedure uncertainties are small enough for that purpose. A point at 21 °C also separates them: A gives 21, B gives 20.9. Choose checks for the intended domain, including interpolation and planned extrapolation, rather than simply repeating the two fitted endpoints. In real work use sufficient independent evidence to characterize residuals and uncertainty; one synthetic pair of curves is not a universal validation design.

Unmodeled dependence is another source of apparent precision. A shared offset, sensor response time, position difference or slowly changing ambient can leave residual structure that independent-readout formulas miss. Repeated numbers from the same process do not automatically provide independent information. Preserve time/order/location metadata and compare predicted patterns with observed ones. The thermal conductance study assumes one shared additive offset; a sensor-specific slope or nonlinearity need not cancel in a difference.

Do not count a physical contribution twice. If a repeated-condition spread already contains a varying environmental effect, adding the same effect again as an independent term overstates its role. Conversely, a fixed unknown common offset may leave no trace in that spread. Name each physical cause and its evidence before combining terms. An arbitrary extra uncertainty term cannot substitute for investigating a known disagreement.

**Read and work:** [Developing and using measurement models — JCGM GUM-6](../metrology/sources/GUM-Models.lingua#r_3AAJPGTHAAF72R05SJ1VJ7HN46), clauses 8.5 and 12, and the next shared model-discrepancy Record. Map each input of the conductance study to its evidence and propose a rival model. State what an independent check would show, what remains unresolved if it agrees, and which domain the claim covers. Numerical agreement establishes consistency with that check; it does not prove the model uniquely true.

**Continue:** [Model discrepancy, double counting, and sensitivity to assumptions](../metrology/uncertainty/Model-discrepancy-and-robustness.lingua#r_13PJ61YH29120EKV42ZT1SQTG3).

